<http://data.silknow.org/object/79f1b21c-6426-323f-9cd8-24536983bbf5>
        a                              ecrm:E22_Man-Made_Object ;
        rdfs:comment                   "woven silk and metal thread with a border of woven silk and metal thread and a facing or binding of plain weave cotton.\nDisplayed behind glass.\nBinding warp: mauve silk; pattern warp: Z-twisted light pink silk.\nWefts: blue silk and silver-gilt strip S-wound on a yellow silk core.\nVery pieced ground; blue ground with a small-scale condensed pattern forming a rigid pattern by the repetition of 2 similar motifs all worked in metal thread: \n[1] a 4-petalled flowerhead with a pair of leaves below and a pair of angled leaves above.  Above this are 2 pairs of serrated leaves pointing towards a mid-point and forming a blue cross with a diamond.  Above this is the base of another 4-petalled flowerhead and above that gold leaves form an X-shape with a vertical blue line in the middle. These motifs repeat in a stripe.\n[2] Next to this, with interconnected elements, is another 4-petalled flowerhead with a pair of leaves above and on either side a downward sweeping trefoil-like flower and a pair of leaves.  Above this are 2 fine gold lines with a pair of horizontal flowers and then a 4-petalled rosette with on either side an upward pointing pair of trefoil flowers and leaves. Above this, 4 serrated leaves point towards the centre.\nFine gold stems and small curls may form an underlying pattern but it is difficult to see.\nBorder: on 3 sides, bias-cut.  There are many pieces from at least two fabrics, including one with a white bird alternating with a round pink blossom, a blue star-like blossom and a green bird.  The main fabric has ameander with a mauve flower, pink blossom, blue flower and white serrated flowers on additional stems. In one section there are three white flowers per stem.\nBinding: probably bias-cut black cotton."@en , "woven silk, 1800-1870, Persian; Flowers"@en ;
        ecrm:P138i_has_representation  <http://data.silknow.org/image/96c1b262-a75b-36c7-8ea7-87b40658653d> , <http://data.silknow.org/image/1c787459-8f9f-378f-9500-2c7e60810068> ;
        ecrm:P3_has_note               "woven silk, 1800-1870, Persian; Flowers"@en , "woven silk and metal thread with a border of woven silk and metal thread and a facing or binding of plain weave cotton.\nDisplayed behind glass.\nBinding warp: mauve silk; pattern warp: Z-twisted light pink silk.\nWefts: blue silk and silver-gilt strip S-wound on a yellow silk core.\nVery pieced ground; blue ground with a small-scale condensed pattern forming a rigid pattern by the repetition of 2 similar motifs all worked in metal thread: \n[1] a 4-petalled flowerhead with a pair of leaves below and a pair of angled leaves above.  Above this are 2 pairs of serrated leaves pointing towards a mid-point and forming a blue cross with a diamond.  Above this is the base of another 4-petalled flowerhead and above that gold leaves form an X-shape with a vertical blue line in the middle. These motifs repeat in a stripe.\n[2] Next to this, with interconnected elements, is another 4-petalled flowerhead with a pair of leaves above and on either side a downward sweeping trefoil-like flower and a pair of leaves.  Above this are 2 fine gold lines with a pair of horizontal flowers and then a 4-petalled rosette with on either side an upward pointing pair of trefoil flowers and leaves. Above this, 4 serrated leaves point towards the centre.\nFine gold stems and small curls may form an underlying pattern but it is difficult to see.\nBorder: on 3 sides, bias-cut.  There are many pieces from at least two fabrics, including one with a white bird alternating with a round pink blossom, a blue star-like blossom and a green bird.  The main fabric has ameander with a mauve flower, pink blossom, blue flower and white serrated flowers on additional stems. In one section there are three white flowers per stem.\nBinding: probably bias-cut black cotton."@en ;
